{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And behold, HaShem stood upon it.</b> It also alludes to Jacob, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 82:6), that the patriarchs are the chariot of the Shekhinah. And since he is the fourth leg, it may say precisely ״נִצָּב עָלָיו״ — [standing] upon him — more so than upon Abraham and Isaac, for he is the principal establishment of the Throne." "<b>Elohim of Abraham your father, etc.</b> The reason it says ״your father״ seems unnecessary, and it is also a fine point why it says ״your father״ regarding Abraham and did not say ״Abraham and Isaac your fathers.״ It intended to exclude Esau from the inheritance of Abraham, making Jacob the heir of Abraham — not that the inheritance would pass to Isaac and from him Jacob would inherit it, for if so the inheritance would reach Esau. And there is no concern regarding Ishmael, for I have already written (Or HaChaim on Genesis 16:21) that the status of a slave applies to him, as it is written (Genesis 21:13) ״the son of the maidservant״ and not ״your son,״ and his person is acquired by Isaac; and Esau has no claim upon him, because the inheritance of Abraham was given to Jacob and not to Esau." "And although an apostate son inherits his father by Torah law (Kiddushin 18a), here, when HaShem gave gifts and blessings to Abraham and the like, He gave them to him on condition that after him they would go to Jacob, as He said (Genesis 21:12) ״כִּי בְיִצְחָק יִקָּרֵא לְךָ זָרַע״ (Nedarim 31a). And although the term ״seed״ would not be detrimental to inheritance — for one inherits even if he is not called his seed — behold, we find that disqualified seed is also called seed, as is stated in Sanhedrin (64b): ״כִּי מִזַּרְעוֹ נָתַן לַמֹּלֶךְ״ — to include disqualified seed; see, it is called seed. Rather, plain ״seed״ refers to one who is fit, and when it excludes Esau here so that he is not called seed, it excludes even disqualified seed — he is not to be called such — and from the inference you learn that he has no inheritance therein. And as HaShem revealed in the end, that He gives Jacob all the blessings of Abraham. And what they learned in the Talmud (Kiddushin 18a) from the verse ״כִּי יְרֻשָּׁה לְעֵשָׂו נָתַתִּי״ etc. — that means that his sons inherited from Esau, not that Esau inherited, heaven forbid, for it is obvious that he has no portion or inheritance in Abraham."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:21.608224136Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}